Title:
Leveraging in-depth Windows Internals for Advanced EDR Evasion

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This article is a deep, Windows-internals-driven walkthrough of building a stealthy user-mode loader in Rust and evaluating it primarily against Microsoft Defender for Endpoint (MDE).  
- It explains how EDRs collect telemetry (user-mode API hooks, ETW, kernel callbacks/minifilters, call stack inspection) and why some common evasion patterns fail once kernel telemetry is involved.  
- The author implements low-level primitives: resolving NTDLL via the PEB, parsing the Export Address Table to locate NT functions, extracting syscall numbers from syscall stubs, and performing direct/indirect syscalls to bypass userland hooks and reduce static indicators.  
- It then pivots from noisy remote-thread injection (kernel callback visibility) to Early Bird APC Queue injection using NtCreateUserProcess + NtQueueApcThread + NtResumeThread, plus ETW patching, PPID spoofing, and process mitigation policies to block non-Microsoft DLLs.  
- Call stack spoofing is covered in detail (unwind info/.pdata parsing, gadget selection, stack frame fabrication) and its practical limits are demonstrated (MDE still sees process creation via kernel callbacks).  
- The final sections integrate C2 shellcode (msfvenom), discuss static-analysis friction (API hashing/entropy tradeoffs), and briefly survey modern loader trends (RDI/sRDI/UdRL/PIC/Crystal Palace).  
- Useful for red teamers, malware developers, and defenders wanting concrete insight into which telemetry sources remain unavoidable and what “stealth” looks like in practice on modern Windows.

Technical Focus:
- PEB/TEB parsing and module enumeration (resolving ntdll.dll without WinAPI)
- PE internals: EAT/IAT parsing, RVAs, section layout, loader behavior
- Direct vs indirect syscalls (SSN extraction, syscall stubs, trampolines)
- ETW patching (EtwEventWrite RET patch) and telemetry tradeoffs
- Early Bird APC Queue injection (NtCreateUserProcess, NtQueueApcThread, NtResumeThread)
- Call stack spoofing/stack unwinding (.pdata, UNWIND_INFO, RtlLookupFunctionEntry, gadgets)

Use Cases:
- Building user-mode loaders that minimize userland-hook telemetry
- Evaluating which injection primitives trigger kernel callbacks in EDRs
- Implementing Early Bird APC injection with PPID spoofing for stealthier process trees
- Researching call stack inspection evasion and unwind-metadata-based spoofing
- Developing Rust-based offensive tooling that avoids common WinAPI resolution paths

Keywords:
Windows internals, EDR evasion, Microsoft Defender for Endpoint, Rust malware development, PEB, TEB, ntdll.dll, PE format, Export Address Table, Import Address Table, SSN, direct syscalls, indirect syscalls, syscall trampoline, ETW, EtwEventWrite, kernel callbacks, PsSetCreateThreadNotifyRoutine, process injection, Early Bird APC, NtCreateUserProcess, NtQueueApcThread, NtResumeThread, PPID spoofing, PROCESS_CREATION_MITIGATION_POLICY_BLOCK_NON_MICROSOFT_BINARIES_ALWAYS_ON, call stack inspection, call stack spoofing, UNWIND_INFO, .pdata, RtlLookupFunctionEntry, KiUserApcDispatcher, RtlUserThreadStart, AMSI, BYOVD